[?2004l[?2004h> PROBLEM ANALYSIS:
[?2004l[?2004h> ================
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The intermittent "gzip: stdin: not in gzip format" errors were caused by
[?2004l[?2004h> locale-dependent glob expansion in restore.sh.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ROOT CAUSE:
[?2004l[?2004h> -----------
[?2004l[?2004h> The .bashrc file contains a locale rotation mechanism that alternates
[?2004l[?2004h> LC_COLLATE between 'C' and 'en_US.UTF-8' on each shell invocation.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The original restore.sh used:
[?2004l[?2004h>   cat "$CHUNKS_DIR"/chunk_* | gunzip | tar xf - -C "$OUTPUT_DIR"
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Glob expansion (chunk_*) order depends on LC_COLLATE:
[?2004l[?2004h> - LC_COLLATE=C: ASCII ordering (uppercase before lowercase)
[?2004l[?2004h> - LC_COLLATE=en_US.UTF-8: Case-insensitive ordering
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> When chunks are concatenated in the wrong order, the gzip stream is
[?2004l[?2004h> corrupted, causing gunzip to fail.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> SOLUTION:
[?2004l[?2004h> ---------
[?2004l[?2004h> Fixed restore.sh to use explicit sorting:
[?2004l[?2004h>   find "$CHUNKS_DIR" -name 'chunk_*' -type f | sort | xargs cat | gunzip | tar xf - -C "$OUTPUT_DIR"
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The 'sort' command uses consistent ordering regardless of LC_COLLATE,
[?2004l[?2004h> ensuring chunks are always concatenated in the correct sequence.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> VERIFICATION:
